course: dictionary
lesson: 242
title: Almost: very nearly, but not quite
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Almost means very nearly, but not completely. «It is almost ten o'clock» means it is not ten yet, but it is very close to ten.
explain: Almost can also mean that something nearly happened but did not. «I almost fell» means I was close to falling, but I did not fall. To check, put nearly in the same place. If the sentence still makes sense, almost means much the same.
explain: In maths, almost tells you a number is just below another. «Almost 100» could be 98 or 99, but not 100 or more. Words such as «almost always» and «almost never» show how often something happens.
fact: Almost means very nearly, but not completely.
fact: «Almost» can show a close call: «The car almost hit him» means the car missed him.
fact: In maths, «almost 100» could be 98 or 99, but never 100 or more.
fact: «Almost» often goes before words such as «always», «never», «all» and «there»: «almost always», «almost never», «almost all», «almost there».
fact: «Mercury» has almost no atmosphere, so it has very little air to keep heat in.
exercise: «It is almost ten o'clock.» What does almost mean here? | Exactly ten o'clock | Past eleven o'clock | Ten minutes ago | *Very nearly ten, but not quite
exercise: «I almost fell.» Did the speaker fall? | Yes, they fell over | Yes, but they were not hurt | Nobody knows | *No, they came close to falling but did not
exercise: Which number could «almost 100» mean? | *98 | 101 | 100 | 120
test: «The car almost hit him.» What does this tell you? | The car was parked by the road | He was hit by the car | Nobody was on the road | *He was in danger, but the car did not hit him
test: Which phrase means that something happens nearly every time? | almost empty | *almost always | almost never | almost dark
exam: «The tank is almost empty.» What does this mean? | Painted green | *Nearly empty, but with a little left | Empty for a long time | Completely full
